3e76ff6c2251409d67bd4d44714df545c2e1406f35c243db21f791e2a3cb52" "q-106404/selection/9fdffdcefa2669090517ba484e29f6ed00149d1445e92f5c983188190ce91794" "q-106404/selection/83d70e0048515696e5c8563bea8d4b2c3cdf15ab2aa67edacd2c421ea082130e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106404" :question/id #uuid "30cbcaa6-ed27-5b10-b6d2-804bd939ad37" :question/math-academy-id "q-106404" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/dbaad877ff577039a288011f904d856bec1417d995113c4c137f7ac536d44968.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/5a028284d29850a9561a589c0e9fbf85e2e10e4e7a23d4ab46a765f492955b4f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106404/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106404"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79280/selection/5bda35dc7fabc5f84da2118d36bbe7c90786c71810c1d882ae9f6c6935b490ae" :answer/id #uuid "80273472-98e2-512e-bd9a-143bac918094" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79280/selection/f50abb079f365a16a9ff95d77296d9830a698ba4fbacfae3a775f393bfed6a3b" :answer/id #uuid "2d905db8-ccb6-5df3-9853-0d1bd69af0ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79280/selection/3a0472f96eccaa2c8746fb82da346e062d6cd3ce394434f334ccfd0c4ee2173b" :answer/id #uuid "d5b7f5db-ea64-5d93-ad97-60e3e0cf3236" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79280/selection/83d70e0048515696e5c8563bea8d4b2c3cdf15ab2aa67edacd2c421ea082130e" :answer/id #uuid "38e94396-4463-570f-b4b7-92725f5049fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79280/selection/254f7dc7c93945f5d800c08002268b921573aada24b982a69940059f541d28cc" :answer/id #uuid "23ae24e3-4df9-5efc-aa40-4228dad03f3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79280/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6afd19c7-84bb-5bec-9658-2de15362cf1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79280/selection/254f7dc7c93945f5d800c08002268b921573aada24b982a69940059f541d28cc" :answer-field/choices ["q-79280/selection/5bda35dc7fabc5f84da2118d36bbe7c90786c71810c1d882ae9f6c6935b490ae" "q-79280/selection/f50abb079f365a16a9ff95d77296d9830a698ba4fbacfae3a775f393bfed6a3b" "q-79280/selection/3a0472f96eccaa2c8746fb82da346e062d6cd3ce394434f334ccfd0c4ee2173b" "q-79280/selection/83d70e0048515696e5c8563bea8d4b2c3cdf15ab2aa67edacd2c421ea082130e" "q-79280/selection/254f7dc7c93945f5d800c08002268b921573aada24b982a69940059f541d28cc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79280" :question/id #uuid "0887763c-3065-51d5-8701-cd91cb07d032" :question/math-academy-id "q-79280" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/7540559d86e836d869173da11ca0f7befba8ead07c844be1c9708591fb372042.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/30bb7c769ff0cbabcb13e3f48aaad1e4c8c34ded6b571858248eb9819eb2bbe4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-79280/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79280"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106715/selection/51c8b210bd855fff90ffc7c190027fda5e3019bdf2956d27620cde5b7a993bf9" :answer/id #uuid "5089e03e-4676-58fe-9773-3d6d9fd26794" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106715/selection/c9f54a9824082183ca9062fdb057fff3c18006d38041767af8198174c8fea963" :answer/id #uuid "6cc68bba-d0c4-5ad3-81d1-cd27f1c5065a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106715/selection/de3475c7c5d6066bb0c98c33ac25c063262ca191ca33703461d719d6d56f8a04" :answer/id #uuid "d6413914-a9e4-54a0-839f-e122921f51e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106715/selection/9d988a462a7f2a1d6bc7652a55e6289629356acc481d0fd50de39dc26544a0f8" :answer/id #uuid "50418daa-4152-5779-8cb8-0144581a7f6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106715/selection/3b9483c9771280da0390d9b48c02cd8f1c7e4f2219475655dc90446b99c894bc" :answer/id #uuid "49fd921e-91e3-50d9-a815-9898ece36c19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106715/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8a321ea-ad40-501f-8030-fb34017b248e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106715/selection/51c8b210bd855fff90ffc7c190027fda5e3019bdf2956d27620cde5b7a993bf9" :answer-field/choices ["q-106715/selection/51c8b210bd855fff90ffc7c190027fda5e3019bdf2956d27620cde5b7a993bf9" "q-106715/selection/c9f54a9824082183ca9062fdb057fff3c18006d38041767af8198174c8fea963" "q-106715/selection/de3475c7c5d6066bb0c98c33ac25c063262ca191ca33703461d719d6d56f8a04" "q-106715/selection/9d988a462a7f2a1d6bc7652a55e6289629356acc481d0fd50de39dc26544a0f8" "q-106715/selection/3b9483c9771280da0390d9b48c02cd8f1c7e4f2219475655dc90446b99c894bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106715" :question/id #uuid "db659393-c5ee-5124-a2e0-8e5c4f1f44ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-106715" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106715/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106715"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-79375"] :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 \\\\ y-1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-327808/field-1/a2cdadce040fc5ee01d00c9fc818dd33352b927bea2ac57cb0cbb7a7390b3478" :answer/id #uuid "13dc05ed-4d2a-54f7-9143-1df7dfaf56b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{96}+\\frac{y^2}{50}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-327808/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a68d4dad-6dbc-5905-9104-5cbbef20ce2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-327808/field-1/a2cdadce040fc5ee01d00c9fc818dd33352b927bea2ac57cb0cbb7a7390b3478" :answer-field/choices ["q-327808/field-1/a2cdadce040fc5ee01d00c9fc818dd33352b927bea2ac57cb0cbb7a7390b3478"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-327808" :question/id #uuid "3a17ad85-75b3-57c6-af87-8cba05167eb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-327808" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{96} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{50} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-327808/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-327808"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106406/selection/6ba9e0925630577f390169b270bcdd12dfdc80763cecc92fa4d53901bbbe22a4" :answer/id #uuid "d1619ef5-bb2e-58c9-8361-e4c22b35dd95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106406/selection/a0685a43da352a78d816cd1279bc2f0e0b1a009aa53c0cda1a7b8652bd6b68b3" :answer/id #uuid "1f096eca-93a6-5e7a-a73c-8a6d3e69b7a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106406/selection/2c2b861dc9fb2fbde6d2205218ef0aa83ca5a8366d4dd5b9b89dd111338df83c" :answer/id #uuid "334bb100-678c-551c-b431-5ad508a48042" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106406/selection/17cb60bcd1bc2194e8b59136c2cec0811d6cc199aea98365ab6c57cdce60c888" :answer/id #uuid "698cb1f5-9f40-502e-9c6c-d125f336a37d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106406/selection/ccdbd396d1688a816c84e4d5519bab3d504e28ff6916549c42db03928f239708" :answer/id #uuid "994a8f87-54d3-56eb-a26c-246d8d55a67b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106406/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6788439-9f18-5be1-81dc-e9244ab289ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106406/selection/2c2b861dc9fb2fbde6d2205218ef0aa83ca5a8366d4dd5b9b89dd111338df83c" :answer-field/choices ["q-106406/selection/6ba9e0925630577f390169b270bcdd12dfdc80763cecc92fa4d53901bbbe22a4" "q-106406/selection/a0685a43da352a78d816cd1279bc2f0e0b1a009aa53c0cda1a7b8652bd6b68b3" "q-106406/selection/2c2b861dc9fb2fbde6d2205218ef0aa83ca5a8366d4dd5b9b89dd111338df83c" "q-106406/selection/17cb60bcd1bc2194e8b59136c2cec0811d6cc199aea98365ab6c57cdce60c888" "q-106406/selection/ccdbd396d1688a816c84e4d5519bab3d504e28ff6916549c42db03928f239708"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106406" :question/id #uuid "4db2471c-43eb-5703-bc68-40fdaaff995e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106406" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 9{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106406/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106406"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6814"] :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6816"] :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6818"] :question/problem "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
